      SUBROUTINE HTTP.GET(URL, RESP)
* mvx-lang/http-curl — GET a URL, returning the status AND the body.
* Copyright (C) 2026 Gordon Heydon.  GPL-2.0-only (see ../LICENSE).
*
*   RESP<1>  the HTTP status (0 when the transport itself failed)
*   RESP<2>  response headers — reserved, see the note on the transport below
*   RESP<3>  the response body
*
* THIS IS A LAYER, NOT A SECOND TRANSPORT.  The virtual name "http" is the API;
* the virtual name "curl" is the transport underneath it, provided by
* mvx-lang/curl-cmd (the OS curl command) or mvx-lang/curl (libcurl in process).
* Everything platform-shaped -- the shell quoting, jBASE having no shell at all,
* UniVerse needing SH -c -- lives down there and is not repeated here.  That is
* the whole point of the split: one HTTP API, either transport, four platforms.
*
* WHY THE NAMES DIFFER FROM THE TRANSPORT'S.  The transport exports HTTPGET and
* HTTPGETFILE.  If this package exported those too they would collide in the
* catalog -- one shared library on jBASE, one global catalog on UniData, one VOC
* per account on UniVerse -- and whichever was cataloged last would win.  Worse,
* this program calls them, so it would eventually call itself.  So the API takes
* its own names and the transport keeps its own.
*
* STATUS AND BODY TOGETHER, which neither transport call gives on its own:
* HTTPGET returns a body and no status, HTTPGETFILE a status and no body.  So
* fetch to a temp file for the status, then read the file back for the body.
$INCLUDE MVPKG.INC PLATFORM.H
*
* The transport is resolved by NAME at run time.  jBASE has no
* `DEFFUN ... CALLING`, and on UniVerse a leading "*" names the GLOBAL catalog
* while packages are cataloged LOCAL -- so each platform gets the spelling that
* resolves there.  Flat guards: an $IFDEF nested inside an $ELSE compiles on
* UniData and fails on UniVerse.
$IFDEF JBASE
      DEFFUN HTTPGETFILE(A, B)
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UDT
      DEFFUN HTTPGETFILE(A, B) CALLING "*HTTPGETFILE"
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UV
      DEFFUN HTTPGETFILE(A, B) CALLING "HTTPGETFILE"
$ENDIF
$IFDEF MVX
* MVX links a package rather than cataloging it, so the transport's name is
* resolved at link time and needs no CALLING clause.  NOT ENABLED YET: nothing
* provides `curl` on MVX -- mvx-lang/curl carries a libcurl binding and native
* mvx sources, but its manifest declares udt only -- so this arm is written and
* untested, and the manifest leaves mvx out until there is a transport to test
* it against.
      DEFFUN HTTPGETFILE(A, B)
$ENDIF
      RESP = ""
      RESP<1> = 0
      RESP<2> = ""
      RESP<3> = ""
      IF TRIM(URL) = "" THEN RETURN
      HGTMP = "/tmp/httpcurl_" : @USERNO
      HGST = HTTPGETFILE(URL, HGTMP)
      RESP<1> = HGST
      HGBODY = ""
      GOSUB HGREAD
      RESP<3> = HGBODY
      GOSUB HGRM
      RETURN

* HGREAD — HGBODY = the whole of HGTMP.  UniVerse has no OSREAD: it parses
* `OSREAD X FROM p` as a variable being assigned and says so as "Variable Name
* (UNDEFINED) unexpected", so OPENSEQ/READSEQ is the equivalent there.
HGREAD:
$IFDEF UV
      OPENSEQ HGTMP TO HGF THEN
         HGDONE = 0
         LOOP UNTIL HGDONE DO
            READSEQ HGLINE FROM HGF THEN
               IF HGBODY = "" THEN HGBODY = HGLINE ELSE HGBODY := CHAR(10) : HGLINE
            END ELSE HGDONE = 1
         REPEAT
         CLOSESEQ HGF
      END
$ENDIF
$IFDEF JBASE
      OSREAD HGBODY FROM HGTMP ELSE HGBODY = ""
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UDT
      OSREAD HGBODY FROM HGTMP ELSE HGBODY = ""
$ENDIF
$IFDEF MVX
      HGBODY = OSREAD(HGTMP)
      IF STATUS() # 0 THEN HGBODY = ""
$ENDIF
      RETURN

* HGRM — drop the temp file.  jBASE's EXECUTE runs an OS command directly with
* no shell, so there is nothing to quote and quotes would arrive literally;
* UniVerse needs SH -c; UniData takes "!".
HGRM:
$IFDEF JBASE
      EXECUTE 'rm -f ' : HGTMP CAPTURING HGOUT
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UDT
      EXECUTE '!rm -f "' : HGTMP : '"' CAPTURING HGOUT
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UV
      EXECUTE "SH -c " : CHAR(34) : "rm -f " : HGTMP : CHAR(34) CAPTURING HGOUT
$ENDIF
$IFDEF MVX
* OSDELETE is a FUNCTION here, not a subroutine -- `CALL OSDELETE(p)` does not
* compile.  Its result is 1 when the file went; nothing depends on that.
      HGGONE = OSDELETE(HGTMP)
$ENDIF
      RETURN
